\question \emph{Section 1.4, Exercise 1}

Let
\[
A=\{1,3,12,35\},\qquad
B=\{3,7,12,20\},
\]
and let
\[
C=\{x\mid x\text{ is a prime number}\}.
\]

\begin{parts}

\part Find $A\cap B$.

The elements common to both $A$ and $B$ are $3$ and $12$. Therefore,
\[
\boxed{A\cap B=\{3,12\}}.
\]

\part Find $(A\cup B)\setminus C$.

First,
\[
A\cup B=\{1,3,7,12,20,35\}.
\]
Of these elements, $3$ and $7$ are prime, so they must be removed. Thus,
\[
\boxed{(A\cup B)\setminus C=\{1,12,20,35\}}.
\]

\part Find $A\cup(B\setminus C)$.

The prime elements of $B$ are $3$ and $7$, so
\[
B\setminus C=\{12,20\}.
\]
Therefore,
\[
A\cup(B\setminus C)
=\{1,3,12,35\}\cup\{12,20\}
=\boxed{\{1,3,12,20,35\}}.
\]

\part Are any of the sets listed above disjoint? Are any subsets of another?

The sets $\{3,12\}$ and $\{1,12,20,35\}$ are not disjoint because they both contain $12$.
The set $\{1,12,20,35\}$ and $\{1,3,12,20,35\}$ are not disjoint because they have several elements in common.

Also,
\[
\{1,12,20,35\}\subseteq\{1,3,12,20,35\}.
\]
Thus, the second set is a subset of the third set.

\end{parts}

