\question \emph{Section 1.4, Exercise 14}

Use Venn diagrams to show the associative law for symmetric difference:
\[
A\triangle(B\triangle C)=(A\triangle B)\triangle C.
\]

Recall that $A\triangle B$ consists of the elements that belong to exactly one of $A$ and $B$.

A three-set Venn diagram can be divided into eight regions. The regions that belong to either side of the equation are precisely those in which an odd number of the sets $A,B,C$ contain the element. Thus, both sides shade the same four regions.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.05]
    % Left diagram
    \begin{scope}
        \draw (-1.7,-1.4) rectangle (1.7,1.4);
        \draw (-0.55,0) circle (0.9);
        \draw (0.55,0) circle (0.9);
        \draw (0,0.65) circle (0.9);

        \node at (-0.95,-1.15) {$A$};
        \node at (0.95,-1.15) {$B$};
        \node at (0,1.35) {$C$};

        % Labels for odd-membership regions
        \node at (-0.55,-0.18) {$1$};
        \node at (0.55,-0.18) {$1$};
        \node at (0,0.78) {$1$};
        \node at (0,-0.05) {$1$};

        \node at (0,-1.8) {$A\triangle(B\triangle C)$};
    \end{scope}

    % Right diagram
    \begin{scope}[xshift=4.4cm]
        \draw (-1.7,-1.4) rectangle (1.7,1.4);
        \draw (-0.55,0) circle (0.9);
        \draw (0.55,0) circle (0.9);
        \draw (0,0.65) circle (0.9);

        \node at (-0.95,-1.15) {$A$};
        \node at (0.95,-1.15) {$B$};
        \node at (0,1.35) {$C$};

        \node at (-0.55,-0.18) {$1$};
        \node at (0.55,-0.18) {$1$};
        \node at (0,0.78) {$1$};
        \node at (0,-0.05) {$1$};

        \node at (0,-1.8) {$(A\triangle B)\triangle C$};
    \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

The same four regions are selected in both diagrams. Therefore,
\[
\boxed{A\triangle(B\triangle C)=(A\triangle B)\triangle C}.
\]

